WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.819 align:start position:0%
 
what do you make of this news with  regard to Teamsters and potentially uber  drivers forming a union well look I  think that this is sort of an emerging  market space and there's a natural  give-and-take  I think the announcement today the  settlement is a win-win I think it's a  good step in the right direction  I think it's uber saying we're gonna be  doing more to sort of self-regulate but  I do think that the market has some fits  and starts here but I see this as an  overall win for uber now uber and the  CEO Travis kalanick have emphasized they  believe drivers value independence value  their flexibility despite the this  class-action lawsuit however a lawyer  for the driver said today no court no  court has decided here whether Ober  drivers are employees or independent  contractors and that debate will not end  here this case stands as a stern warning  to companies who play fast and loose  with classifying their workforce as  independent contractors who do not  receive the benefits of the wage laws  and other employee protections so of  course they've just settled this but  again no court has actually made a  decision here professor you know what do  you think of the broader implications  for the sharing economy and for uber  going forward well as far as uber is  concerned this is a big win for them as  you've pointed out Uber's biggest source  of valuation risk is uncertainty around  worker classification this is a big step  in the direction of uber being able to  sort of like you know put that issue  aside and as a consequence I think that  the hundred million dollars they're  going to make up sort of multifold  through an increase in the evaluation I  think overall this is good for the  sharing economy I'm sure that there are  situations in which it makes sense to  classify sharing economy providers as  employees but with uber drivers and lyft  drivers I don't think this is one of  those cases I do think however it's  really important that we start to think  about a way in which we can fund  benefits for these drivers and for other  providers in the sharing economy  it's not going to come all from uber  it's not all going to come from the  government it's not going to come from  the drivers themselves there has to be a  partnership model and you know I think  when we look back on this case  we're not going to remember the details  of the settlement but we're going to see  it as the event that triggered this  conversation about the need to protect  the workforce of the future which is  going to be largely freelance now one of  the interesting parts of this settlement  is that drivers can now say hey tips  aren't required or expected but they are  very much appreciated and one of the  great things about the uber experience  has been to press a button get in get  out and not have to worry about a tip or  worry about doing anything else you Paul  what do you make of that could that  significantly change the uber experience  so I think people have a newly ingrained  habit of just uber a ride they like the  idea that it just sort of shows up like  a teleporter you know whenever they they  hit this they're out of the app on their  phone but at the same time you know that  is not an insignificant issue you know I  liked it you liked it we all liked the  idea that it took away this kind of end  of ride decision-making about what I  have to do with respect to a tip which  sounds silly but it was really important  and now the problem is is having  reinstituted that at the back end what  happens if I don't tip does that mean  that I as a passenger get a bad rating  and that the next time I call for an  uber an uber doesn't come and get me  because I didn't tip last time it  introduces a weird new source of  uncertainty because you sort of backdoor  reintroducing this thing that we thought  we got rid of which was this nasty sort  of subjective business of tipping and  cab rides now rashon you know what have  you heard from drivers about do they do  they want tips do they do they even want  to put the cells out there like that  am I talking to drivers and I've talked  to a couple I think everybody's happy to  get tips obviously it's a great extra  source of income and it sort of shows  the recognition and appreciation for the  work that they're doing I do think that  uber is going to over and lyft and  others will probably have to sort of  integrate or iterate their product in  order to accommodate that and how they  choose to do it whether it's at the  point where you leave the car or the  point where the the receipt comes back  and you can see the total  that remains to be seen but I'm sure  drivers will be happy to have tips  included or added now it's clear that  laws still need to change in order to  accommodate the sharing economy so Arun  what are some of the hurdles going  forward for uber and other sharing  economy companies well I think what's  really going to create an opportunity  for uber for lyft for the other sharing  economy platforms is if we successfully  create a safe harbor in which they can  demonstrate that they are willing to  participate in sort of providing a  safety net for their drivers if there  isn't the threat of this kind of you  know sort of class-action lawsuit and so  I don't see the case as having  eliminated the threat of like you know  sort of you know additional costs that  may come from different worker  classification but I do see it as laying  the foundations for being able to say  that now let's go ahead let's create the  safe harbor and let us show you that  like you know we are willing to sort of  pay for part of the benefits that these  drivers the face of our brands the  providers who were sort of fighting with  each other to retain to bring to our  platform give us the space to be able to  sort of offer them different things that  they want as a retention strategy and so  you know I mean companies have done this  all the time with employees and so it's  important that we sort of create a space  for the sharing economy platforms to be  able to sort of do the same kinds of  things

